    "lawCode": "Amd §186-f, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A9777\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to the distrib-\nution   of  monies  collected  from  the  public  safety  communications\nsurcharge\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: This  bill  will  establish  an  annual\nemergency  communications competitive grant program in the amount of $25\nmillion per year, commencing in FY 2010-11.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: New York State Tax Law §186-f is amended\nto dedicate $25 million per year of funds generated by the public safety\ncommunications surcharge to a competitive grant program to assist county\ngovernments with costs associated with the maintenance  and/or  improve-\nment  of  emergency communications systems.   Priority would go to those\ngrant applicants who propose system enhancements that  are  designed  to\nachieve regional or statewide interoperability.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: The public safety communications surcharge was originally\nconceived as a funding mechanism for the statewide operation of a cellu-\nlar  911 telecommunications system and later, for the Statewide Wireless\nNetwork (SWN). After years of developing bid  criteria,  evaluating  bid\nproposals,  design and development, the state has cancelled the contract\nwith M/A-COM for the development of the SWN after  repeated  test  fail-\nures. Any funds expended by the state towards the development of the SWN\n(approximately  $51.5 million) are supposed to be reimbursed by M/A-CON.\nThere has been no indication that 'the state will  re-let  the  contract\nfor a SWN.\n\nCurrently  about $198 million per year is generated by the public safety\ncommunications surcharge.  Of that amount, about $119,460,000 was depos-\nited in the Public Safety Communications Account (PSCA) in  FY  2008-09;\nthe remainder went to the General Fund. Since the Public Safety Communi-\ncations Account   started FY 2008-09 with a balance of $110,377,000, the\naccount  balance  was increased to $229,837,000 with the addition of the\nFY 2008-09 deposit.\n\nIn February 2009, the state's Deficit Reduction Plan (Chapter 2  of  the\nLaws  of  2009)  authorized $65 million to be swept from the PSCA to the\nGeneral Fund. In addition, Tax Law §186-f currently directs the  follow-\ning  annual  payments from the PSCA: $25.5 million for the State Police,\n$1.5 million for the Emergency Services Revolving Loan Fund, $10 million\nfor the Local Reimbursement Grant Program  (for  county  Public  Service\nAnswering  Points) and the costs associated for the SWN, as appropriated\nby the Legislature. After accounting  for  the  statutory  distributions\nfrom  the  PSCA,  the  account  still  had an end of fiscal year 2008-09\nbalance of approximately $54,529,000. It is part of  this  balance  that\nthis  bill seeks to return to county governments to assist them with the\ncost of providing essential emergency services communications systems.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: No fiscal impact to the state General Fund  as  all\nfunds  would  come  from  the  dedicated  Public  Safety  Communications\nAccount.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
